        { "$comment": "P69 (lane cs, fold f26b): `__declspec(dllimport)` — the row above's answer, and `refusedOnDefinition`: a declaration that DEFINES what it declares (a function with its body, an object with its initializer) cannot be one whose definition another image holds. MEASURED (lane cs probe di, 2026-10-20; cl 19.51 and mingw-w64 gcc 13.2.0 on windows-x86_64): an object definition — cl C2491, gcc 'variable definition is marked dllimport'; a function definition — cl C2491, and gcc compiles it into a program that does not link (its call goes through `__imp_f`, which nothing defines): no reference makes a program of either, and this compiler accepted both in silence. FOLD f26b — THE IMPORT ITSELF is not this row's verb but the declaration rows' `linkageSpecifiers` entries of the same two names (`imported`), read by the analyzer per declarator; the redeclaration rules (an import after a definition of the unit refused, a declaration without the import after one with it warned and ending it, an import after a plain `extern` holding) and the refusals of a `static` and a thread-storage import are the analyzer's, each MEASURED (lane cs probes di2-di5). THE GNU SPELLING (`dllimport`, fold f26b): mingw-w64 gcc 13.2.0 imports `__attribute__((dllimport))` exactly as `__declspec(dllimport)` at every position, refuses it on a definition and on a `static`, and answers `__has_attribute(dllimport)` 1; gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3 and Apple clang warn it ignored at every position and answer 0 (lane cs probe di5). So both names are this row's, and `availableObjectFormats` makes them attribute vocabulary of pe alone: elsewhere the plain word is an unknown attribute, warned, and `__has_attribute` answers 0.", "names": ["__declspec(dllimport)", "dllimport"], "availableObjectFormats": ["pe"], "appliesTo": ["variable", "function"], "effect": "none", "refusedOnDefinition": "the attribute says the definition is another image's, which this declaration cannot be" },
